      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's delve into the technical aspects of your tomato greenhouse issue. The symptoms you're describing—small, irregular holes in the older foliage—are indeed characteristic of certain beetle species. One common culprit is the flea beetle (Altica spp.), which can cause similar damage. These beetles are small, about 1-2 mm in length, and they chew small holes in the leaves, which can lead to significant damage over time.\n\nTo detect these beetles, you might need to employ a magnifying glass or a hand lens during your inspections, as they are quite small and can easily be missed with the naked eye. Look for tiny, round holes in the leaves, often in a scattered pattern, as flea beetles tend to move around rather than staying in one place.\n\nAnother possibility is the tomato pinworm (Keiferia lycopersicella), which can cause similar damage. These pests are also small and can be difficult to spot without close examination. They lay their eggs on the underside of leaves, and the larvae bore into the leaves, creating the holes you've observed.\n\nFor a more thorough investigation, consider using pheromone traps to attract and capture beetles. These traps can help you identify the species",
